---
import LandingLayout from '../layouts/LandingLayout.astro';
import ProductBlock from '../components/ProductBlock.astro';
import RelatedLinks from '../components/RelatedLinks.astro';
import { page } from '../data/site';

const related = [
  page('/home-maintenance-records/'),
  page('/home-maintenance-app/'),
  page('/home-inventory-app/'),
  page('/home-warranty-tracker/'),
  page('/home-document-organizer/'),
  page('/resources/how-to-create-a-digital-home-binder/')
];
---
<LandingLayout
  title="Digital Home Binder App for Apple Devices | Around the House"
  description="Create a private digital home binder on iPhone, iPad, and Mac. Keep manuals, warranties, receipts, photos, notes, maintenance history, and important home details with the items they belong to."
  canonicalPath="/digital-home-binder/"
  breadcrumbLabel="Digital home binder"
  h1="A digital home binder organized around your actual home"
  intro="A useful home binder is more than a folder of PDFs. It connects every manual, receipt, warranty, photo, date, and note to the appliance, system, room, or project it describes — so the answer is where you expect it when you need it."
  ctaHeading="Build your binder one item at a time."
  ctaBody="Start with the furnace, water heater, or one appliance whose manual and warranty you never want to hunt for again."
>
  <section>
    <h2>What belongs in a digital home binder?</h2>
    <p>
      A traditional home binder often combines emergency information, appliance paperwork, repair invoices,
      paint colors, contractor details, warranties, and maintenance notes. The challenge is not deciding whether
      those details matter. It is keeping them current and finding the right one quickly.
    </p>
    <ul class="qa-list">
      <li><strong>Appliance and system details</strong><span>Model and serial numbers, filter sizes, installation dates, photos, and notes.</span></li>
      <li><strong>Manuals and documents</strong><span>Owner manuals, estimates, invoices, inspection reports, and other files.</span></li>
      <li><strong>Receipts and warranties</strong><span>Proof of purchase, coverage documents, purchase dates, and expiration dates.</span></li>
      <li><strong>Repair and maintenance history</strong><span>What happened, when it happened, who did the work, and what it cost.</span></li>
      <li><strong>People worth calling again</strong><span>Contractors and service providers kept with the work they performed.</span></li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <ProductBlock
    device="ipad"
    src="/assets/screenshots/new/ipad-binder.webp"
    alt="The digital home Binder in Around The House on iPad, showing organized manuals, receipts, warranties, and other home documents"
  >
    <h2>Documents stay connected to their context</h2>
    <p>
      Around The House includes a Binder for browsing saved documents, but files do not have to live as an
      anonymous list. Attach a furnace manual to the furnace, a roofing invoice to the roof, or a warranty to the
      appliance it covers. You can find the file through the Binder or by opening the item itself.
    </p>
    <p>
      That connection is what turns a collection of scans into a usable record of the home.
    </p>
  </ProductBlock>

  <ProductBlock
    device="iphone"
    flip
    src="/assets/screenshots/new/iphone-warranty-countdown.webp"
    alt="A warranty record in Around The House on iPhone showing its expiration date and the remaining coverage time"
  >
    <h2>Keep the date with the warranty</h2>
    <p>
      A warranty is most useful before it expires. Save its purchase or coverage dates with the document so you
      can see how much time remains without opening a PDF and searching for the fine print.
    </p>
    <p>
      Receipts, photos, and notes can stay alongside the same item, giving you the evidence and identifying
      details you are likely to need for a claim or service call.
    </p>
  </ProductBlock>

  <section>
    <h2>Private by design, without another account</h2>
    <p>
      Around The House does not require a separate web account. Records remain on your Apple devices and, when
      you enable synchronization, in your own private iCloud. There is no advertising profile and no web
      dashboard holding a second copy of your home information.
    </p>
    <p>
      The app is available on iPhone, iPad, and Mac, so details can be captured where the work happens and
      reviewed later on a larger screen.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>A binder that can leave the house with you</h2>
    <p>
      Over time, the same records can support insurance documentation, maintenance planning, a service call, or
      a home handoff. The goal is not to document everything in a weekend. It is to keep each useful detail once,
      in the place where you will look for it later.
    </p>
  </section>

  <RelatedLinks heading="Related home records" items={related} />
</LandingLayout>
